@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-logical-functions Logical Functions
@brief Functions used to compose logical expressions in tests.

@details
Three functions combine the conditions of a check, and report the values
of all their operands when the check fails:
- `mt::all_of (a, b, ...)` is true when all the conditions are true;
- `mt::any_of (a, b, ...)` is true when at least one condition is true,
  including when all are;
- `mt::negate (a)` is true when the condition is false.

`all_of()` and `any_of()` take two or more operands; a failed check shows
them as one group, for example `(1 == 1 and 1 == 1 and 1 == 3)`.

The functions have names, rather than the `&&`, `||`, and `!` operators,
because the built-in operators would return a plain `bool`, which loses
the values of the operands.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check compound conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::all_of (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42),
                        mt::eq (compute_float (), 42.0),
                        mt::lt (compute_answer (), 43)));
});
@endcode

@par Differences from the built-in operators
- All the operands are evaluated before the check; unlike `&&` and `||`,
  the functions do not stop at the first operand that decides the result,
  so do not write `mt::all_of (mt::ne (p, nullptr), mt::eq (*p, 1))`.
- After a `using namespace std`, an unqualified `all_of()` or `any_of()`
  with three or more operands of the same type selects the standard
  algorithm, which does not compile; write `mt::all_of()` and
  `mt::any_of()`.

The overloaded logical operators of the `mt::operators` namespace are
deprecated since 6.0.0, in favour of these functions.
